From 18b3eff24a0773fcf5a10e9e5fb24950b3accbb9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kel Modderman <kelvmod@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 14:03:17 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] [AE] PipeWire: don't wait for a paused stream to drain

Drain() flushes the stream and waits up to 1 s for PipeWire's drained
event. PipeWire only reports drained from its process cycle, which
doesn't run while the stream is paused, and a stream is paused once it
has drained, because Drained() makes it inactive. So when ActiveAE
drains a sink that has already drained, as CloseSink() does when Kodi
exits after playback, Drain() always waits out the full second.

Return straight away unless the stream is streaming. A paused stream has
nothing queued, since AddPackets() activates the stream before it queues
anything.
---
 xbmc/cores/AudioEngine/Sinks/pipewire/AESinkPipewire.cpp | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/xbmc/cores/AudioEngine/Sinks/pipewire/AESinkPipewire.cpp b/xbmc/cores/AudioEngine/Sinks/pipewire/AESinkPipewire.cpp
index 16e93e0..d806fff 100644
--- a/xbmc/cores/AudioEngine/Sinks/pipewire/AESinkPipewire.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/AudioEngine/Sinks/pipewire/AESinkPipewire.cpp
@@ -634,6 +634,10 @@ void CAESinkPipewire::Drain()
 
   PIPEWIRE::CLoopLockGuard lock(loop);
 
+  // A paused stream has nothing queued, and PipeWire only reports drained while streaming
+  if (m_stream->GetState() != PW_STREAM_STATE_STREAMING)
+    return;
+
   m_stream->Flush(true);
 
   int ret = loop.Wait(1s);
